WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:10.600
Here is a puzzle. The tip of a growing axon is a flat fan, and the actin inside it

2
00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:14.660
is never still. Slice through it and look from the side.

3
00:00:15.380 --> 00:00:18.340
New actin joins the filaments at the front edge.

4
00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:24.140
Motor proteins called myosin II pull the whole network back toward the centre.

5
00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:27.140
So how does the edge ever move forward?

6
00:00:27.933 --> 00:00:31.813
That backward movement is called retrograde flow.

7
00:00:32.673 --> 00:00:34.193
The network treadmills.

8
00:00:35.213 --> 00:00:40.453
Actin is added at the front and carried back at the same rate, so the edge goes

9
00:00:40.453 --> 00:00:46.433
nowhere. Think of the actin network as a running engine, and the surface under it,

10
00:00:46.753 --> 00:00:48.273
the substrate, as the road.

11
00:00:49.233 --> 00:00:51.693
The engine runs, but the car stays put.

12
00:00:52.517 --> 00:00:58.697
Now add a clutch. When adhesion molecules link the actin network to the substrate,

13
00:00:59.177 --> 00:01:00.217
the clutch engages.

14
00:01:01.517 --> 00:01:02.897
Retrograde flow slows.

15
00:01:03.877 --> 00:01:07.357
The same actin addition now pushes the edge forward.

16
00:01:08.517 --> 00:01:12.177
Tim Mitchison and Marc Kirschner proposed this in 1988.

17
00:01:12.937 --> 00:01:14.777
It is called the clutch hypothesis.

18
00:01:15.717 --> 00:01:22.577
Put it as a rule. The rate of advance equals the rate of actin addition at the edge,

19
00:01:23.057 --> 00:01:25.497
minus the rate of retrograde flow.

20
00:01:26.537 --> 00:01:31.337
With the clutch off, flow matches addition, and advance is zero.

21
00:01:32.297 --> 00:01:36.177
With the clutch on, flow falls, and advance rises.

22
00:01:37.167 --> 00:01:39.547
Actin makes the advance.

23
00:01:40.647 --> 00:01:42.307
Microtubules make it permanent.

24
00:01:43.667 --> 00:01:47.647
Microtubules are hollow tubes that fill the centre of the growth cone.

25
00:01:48.687 --> 00:01:54.747
Once the edge has moved and the actin ahead has cleared, microtubules extend into

26
00:01:54.747 --> 00:01:55.747
the new territory.

27
00:01:56.627 --> 00:02:01.107
The fan behind them narrows, and that stretch becomes axon shaft.

28
00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:04.960
Now see how a growth cone turns.

29
00:02:05.720 --> 00:02:11.260
If advance is a balance at the edge, turning is a difference in that balance between

30
00:02:11.260 --> 00:02:18.080
the two sides. A cue on one side shifts actin assembly, flow or adhesion there.

31
00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:23.640
Gundersen and Barrett placed a pipette releasing nerve growth factor near chick

32
00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:27.260
sensory neurons. The growth cones turned toward it.

33
00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:31.760
Drugs released onto one side of a growth cone give simple results.

34
00:02:32.540 --> 00:02:36.800
Remove actin on one side, and the growth cone turns away from that side.

35
00:02:37.820 --> 00:02:41.440
Keep the rule. Advance is addition minus flow.

36
00:02:42.540 --> 00:02:44.300
Turning is the difference between sides.
